A||||12||Drako||Has Anyone Here Done A 4.3 Conversion||||1
Z||000000||Drako||11-02-1999||05:11 PM||||I was just wondering if any of you had done a 4.3 conversion. I have the engine already. And im wondering how much you put in overall cash wise on the conversion.....And if you liked the performance of the car afterwards, also do you think it would be worth it to lower the compression ratio, put in a forged crank, and a turbo assembally...pretty much the same engine that runs in the typhoon/syclone...<P>Drako<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||11-03-1999||07:21 PM||||I am also curious about this swap. Seems like it might not be too terrible. Possibly using the Archie kit. Retains the original tranny position and axles (unlike the Zumalt/Pisa kit) but *maybe* the engine is "short" enough that the pulleys don't have to poke out into the wheelwell. (Archie says that this has never created a problem, but it just grates at my sensibilities to imagine whirling belts and pulleys out there in the breeze. Merely an opinion.)||||reg||5
Z||000002||TOM||11-03-1999||08:53 PM||||The belts and pullys are always out in the breeze. Being in the wheel well as long as there is clearance is nothing to worry about||||reg||1
Z||000003||squid||11-04-1999||08:45 PM||||As long as you never drive on dirt roads or gravel and you stay behind a street sweeper. Even stock fieros have a rubber shield between the engine and the wheelwell and they have a multi-v belt not the old less reliable style. And no OEM ever hung them "out in the breeze" in a wheelwell.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||11-04-1999||10:01 PM||||what about fabricating some sort of shield to cover the belts in the wheel well?<P>I've seen the archie kit as well, and I don't like the belts exposed like that either.. but since I can't afford anything like that at the moment, I'm not worrying too much.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||11-05-1999||02:58 PM||||I've heard that some electric water pumps (like drag racers use) will fit a V8 converted Fiero without cutting the wheel well, then you don't need to run a water pump pulley. The down side is that these setups are very expensive and probably don't last as long as a regular pump. I'm seriously considering making a little shield for my Batmobile's pulley and belt, just for rock and debris protection (and peace of mind).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Richard Parnell||11-05-1999||03:21 PM||||Batboy- Is your mobile a V8 archie conversion?  Just curious since I'm planning on doing the same with a small v8.||||reg||11
Z||000007||Phil||11-05-1999||04:57 PM||||Electric waterpumps are usually reserved for drag racing as they only pump about 1/3 the GPM of the mechanical pump. A drag car can be keep cool with a small flow rate because they don't have to run for a long time. To get an electric pump big enough to keep a  car cool for normal operation would take about a 1 or 2 HP motor and your alt. would sap about 3+ HP to drive it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Terrybogin||11-05-1999||08:42 PM||||What kind of water pump is on grand paw's Caprice Classic? It has no pulley and two wires coming from it. Could this be a factory electric water pump that could be used? I believe that some V8 F-bodies use the same pump.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||11-05-1999||08:45 PM||||Is it possible that I could be wrong? ARRRRRG||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||11-06-1999||06:40 AM||||Richard, the main part of the conversion was already done when I bought my car, but it does appear to be a V8 Archie's swap kit.<P>Phil, I think you're right about using the drag racing style electric water pumps. Those are probably not the right pump to use. But, I've heard of some streetrodders using an electric water pump of some sort when they have clearance problems. There must be a street version offered somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Rodrv6||11-08-1999||10:33 PM||||Terry, The water pump on Grandpa's Caprice is driven off of the camshaft through the timing cover.  Might be something to keep in mind for a V-8 swap!||||reg||1
Z||000012||JIM GOOSE||11-10-1999||09:35 AM||||The late model small block with cam drive water pump & frtcover ignition is longer in exactly the WRONG place. This leads to more cutting into the space frame in order to use this engine with its superior features. The shorter 4.3 shouldnt have a problem with clearance but you'll pay a substantial power penalty.||||reg||1
